WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:01.150
(light music)

00:00:01.150 --> 00:00:02.240
- [Narrator] You're probably aware

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:03.800
that different apps on your cellphone

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:05.530
can track your movements.

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:08.100
That location data is
commercially available

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.790
along with other personal information

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:12.480
like your social media
profile, email address,

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.930
and date of birth.

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:15.590
But what the Wall Street
Journal has learned

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:18.400
is that the government has
also bought a commercial data

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.173
and is using it for some
forms of law enforcement.

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.340
- Two components of the
Department of Homeland Security

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:27.830
are using this
app-generated marketing data

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:29.690
for law enforcement purposes.

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:31.850
The fact that there're
millions of cellphones

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:34.370
and cellphone locations in this database

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:37.760
makes it one of the larger
domestic surveillance efforts

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.710
that we've become aware
of in recent years.

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:41.660
It raises a lot of questions
among the Americans

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:42.930
about their privacy

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.250
and what kind of information corporations

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:46.320
are collecting on them,

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.335
and what those corporations are
doing with that information.

00:00:49.335 --> 00:00:50.168
(light music)

00:00:50.168 --> 00:00:51.320
- [Narrator] This is how it works.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:52.210
You get this popup

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:54.910
and agree to let an app use your location.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:56.110
A travel app may want it

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:58.520
to suggest nearby hotels or airports.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:01.470
Rideshare apps want to
know where to pick you up.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.900
But often those apps are
also sharing your location

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.330
with marketers who're using
it for targeted ads, research,

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:09.620
analysis and even reselling it.

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.870
But what we found is that, in some cases,

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:14.700
that consumer data is
being resold to companies

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.650
that buy and sell data for the government.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:19.600
According to people
familiar with the matter

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.960
and documents reviewed by
the Wall Street Journal,

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.670
the Trump administration
has purchased the database

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:27.240
that maps the movements
of millions of cellphones

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:28.280
in the U.S.,

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.437
and it's using it for immigration
and border enforcement.

00:01:31.437 --> 00:01:33.050
(light music)

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:36.690
In 2018, data tracking
contributed to the discovery

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:38.180
of a drug-smuggling tunnel

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:41.140
according to people with
knowledge of the operation.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:44.340
- Sources describe this
one case down in Arizona,

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.000
a border town called, San Luis,

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.820
where a man had allegedly built a tunnel

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:52.790
between his property, which was
an abandoned KFC restaurant,

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:54.350
and the Mexican border.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:56.770
Police say that smugglers
were using this tunnel,

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:58.180
but the interesting thing is,

00:01:58.180 --> 00:01:59.550
when this person was arrested,

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.540
none of the court records indicate

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:05.430
that they found this tunnel
based on cell records.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:07.480
This data was showing cellphone's moving

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.430
from one side of the border to the other

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:11.130
and investigator surmised

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:12.950
there must be an illegal tunnel there

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:14.800
and began further investigation

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.320
that led the arrest of this person.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.010
- [Narrator] In a statement
to the Wall Street Journal,

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:19.957
a CBP spokesman said,

00:02:19.957 --> 00:02:23.937
"While CBP is being provided
access to location information,

00:02:23.937 --> 00:02:26.357
"it is important to note
that such information

00:02:26.357 --> 00:02:29.177
"does not include
cellular phone tower data,

00:02:29.177 --> 00:02:30.727
"is not ingested in bulk,

00:02:30.727 --> 00:02:34.170
"and does not include the
individual user's identity."

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:36.180
The government would not discuss details

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.200
about how it is using the data.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.640
But people familiar with some
of the government efforts

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:43.100
say it is used to generate
investigative leads

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:45.690
about possible illegal border crossings,

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:49.120
and for detection or
tracking of migrant groups.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:51.010
The government's location data efforts

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:52.810
are also described opaquely

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:55.160
in the U.S. Department
of Homeland Security's

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:56.670
Privacy Assessment.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:59.807
One reads, "The goal
is to utilize this data

00:02:59.807 --> 00:03:02.937
"to detect the presence
of - but not identify -

00:03:02.937 --> 00:03:06.547
"individuals in an area
which CBP has identified

00:03:06.547 --> 00:03:08.187
"as an area of interest,

00:03:08.187 --> 00:03:11.517
"consistent with CBP
statutory authorities,

00:03:11.517 --> 00:03:14.022
"federal law, and DHS policy."

00:03:14.022 --> 00:03:16.460
(upbeat music)

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:18.560
- [Woman's Voice] Your average
consumer looks at their phone

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.820
over 150 times a day.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.790
- [Narrator] The marketing
technology ecosystem

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:25.115
has taken off in recent years.

00:03:25.115 --> 00:03:27.040
(upbeat music)

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.350
- [Man's Voice] Marketers create audiences

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:30.180
based on rich device-level attributes

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.810
including location, platform, device type

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:33.780
and app (mumbles).

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.250
All this data has built on

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:39.470
what more than 250 million
mobile consumers do in real life.

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:40.303
Every day,

00:03:40.303 --> 00:03:42.740
- [Narrator] It's big business
and there are many companies

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:45.470
collecting all sorts of different data,

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.330
anything from the speed to a traveling,

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:50.680
to what floor you're on, to
your social media profiles.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:52.940
Some companies known as data brokers

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:56.140
buy different sets of these
data and combine them to create

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:59.910
even more sophisticated
individual digital profiles

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:02.360
while the privacy policies
of these companies say

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:05.410
they do not keep personal
information and explain that,

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:07.940
to them, you were just
a few letters or numbers

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:09.340
known as an Ad-ID.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:12.910
Experts we spoke to say that,
by using large data sets,

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:16.130
it's easy to figure out
who a phone belongs to,

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:17.510
which is one of the reasons

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:20.710
why this data is so
valuable to the government.

00:04:20.710 --> 00:04:23.130
- The location data is some
of the most sensitive data

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:24.070
that exists.

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:25.970
- [Narrator] Alan Butler
is the general counsel

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:28.610
of the Electronic Privacy
Information Center,

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:32.820
a nonprofit research and
advocacy center based in D.C..

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:34.430
- There are a number of different ways

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:36.880
that a company that
obtains these data sets

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:38.670
could identify a person.

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:42.850
One way might be by mapping
the locations over time

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:45.580
because people live in operative patterns,

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:49.220
the companies are more concerned
with discrete connections.

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:52.120
They wanna tie everything
together in an automated way.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:53.490
And the easiest way for them to do that

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:56.050
is by using identifiers, the Ad-ID,

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:57.770
which is gonna connect the dots,

00:04:57.770 --> 00:04:59.880
all the dots of them will add data,

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:03.010
but also email addresses,
Facebook profiles,

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:07.200
or Twitter profiles, or other
sort of app profile data.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:10.060
And so, the more data those
companies can get about you,

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:11.202
the more they can connect it.

00:05:11.202 --> 00:05:12.290
(light music)

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:13.600
- [Narrator] If the
government wanted to get

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:16.060
that kind of data from
your cellphone provider,

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:18.270
they have to get a warrant from a judge.

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:21.150
But because this data is
for sale, for marketers,

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:24.380
they are able to buy it and
access it without a warrant.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:25.850
- Most recently, the Supreme Court

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:28.250
in the Carpenter versus United States case

00:05:29.232 --> 00:05:30.065
restricted the government's ability

00:05:30.065 --> 00:05:33.000
to use a court order
to get commercial data

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:34.130
from the phone company,

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.480
which is the data about where
your phone is connecting

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:37.660
on the cellphone network.

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:40.020
The Supreme Court has
not directly addressed

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:42.390
the purchasing of commercial data,

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:45.410
the federal law doesn't
impose restrictions

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:47.480
and the circumstance under
which the federal government

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:50.820
can collect personal data
under the Privacy Act,

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:52.960
but that also can be tricky

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:55.880
if the company never
actually stores the data

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:57.470
in a government database.

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.810
- [Narrator] Recently, some
companies have taken steps

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:02.300
to alert users about location data.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.690
- This market advertising
data on smartphones

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:06.300
is barely 10 years old

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:08.020
and already we're seeing

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:11.820
that consumers are taking more
control over their privacy

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:15.290
in ways that could've hurt this industry.

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:18.210
Apple and its latest update made it

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:22.420
so that it reminded users how
often apps are tracking them.

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:24.540
And some industry insiders have said

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:27.870
that the amount of
location data has plummeted

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:29.470
since Apple made that change.

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:31.870
Users are becoming
broadly much more aware of

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:34.390
how their phones are tracking
them and taking steps

00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:37.570
to limit what kind of apps have
accessed to their location.

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:40.120
- [Narrator] Critics argue that
if companies have this data,

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:42.830
then why shouldn't the
government also have access to it

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:44.260
for crime prevention.

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:46.530
- I think the reaction that
law enforcement should be able

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:49.670
to get access to data that
the companies have access to,

00:06:49.670 --> 00:06:51.870
it kinda falls into two common traps.

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:53.880
One is that it assumes that the company

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.490
should have access to that data, too,

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:56.500
but even given the fact

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:58.270
that some companies are accessed to data,

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:00.520
I think law enforcement use the data,

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:01.930
it's just a fundamental different thing

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:04.370
because the government
exercises the power of the state

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:07.470
and so we've always had
a special set of rules

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:09.590
for the government's collection of data

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:11.200
and surveillance of individuals.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:12.033
- [Narrator] Right now,

00:07:12.033 --> 00:07:14.430
there is no comprehensive
U.S. privacy law.

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:16.830
The European Union, in 2016,

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:19.940
passed the General Data
Protection Regulation,

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:23.500
which more tightly controls
how European data can be used.

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:25.590
In the U.S., there are ongoing efforts

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:29.130
to introduce tougher national
privacy laws in Congress.

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:31.770
- Programs like this are
certainly going to fuel the call

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:34.510
for a comprehensive privacy
law in the United States

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:37.973
that limits the way
corporations can share data

00:07:37.973 --> 00:07:39.430
that they collect on users.

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:41.010
California has taken some steps

00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:44.100
towards passing some
greater privacy protections,

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:48.160
but federally, there's no
law that stops or limits

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:51.890
what corporations can do
with the data they collect

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:54.640
on their users in this particular market.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:56.913
That's how it ended up in
the hands of the government.

